+### Deep-research protocol (Phase 2 — sequential, after Phase 0 verification and Phase 1 paper acquisition)
+
+Accept a research topic and a central scientific question from the user. Conduct an iterative literature investigation within configured operational limits.
+
+1. Scope the question (Stage A): identify main physical concepts, competing theoretical frameworks, related terminology, mathematical structures, applicable regimes/scales, and relevant synonyms. Do not assume the user's preferred hypothesis is correct.
+
+2. Generate targeted searches (Stage B): invoke `physctl paper search --query ... --limit ...` using both `arXiv` and `OpenAlex` sources (via the `internal/paper` layer implemented in Phase 1). Generate at least 5 distinct search strategies: foundational literature, modern theoretical developments, alternative explanations, serious technical objections/rebuttals, and relevant experimental/observational constraints. Do not rely on a single query.
+
+3. Citation expansion (Stage C): for each important discovered paper, invoke `physctl paper related --limit ... <paper-id>` (OpenAlex citation/reference endpoints). Use bounded traversal (default `--limit 10`, budget: 3 rounds maximum). Preserve relationship sources. Deduplicate by DOI/arXiv identifier. Never silently discard conflicting metadata.
+
+4. Download and cache (Stage D): for papers with open-access full text (`IsOA` or `open_access.oa_url` confirmed), invoke `physctl paper fetch --version v1 <paper-id>`. Verify the local `manifest.json` records: `paper_id`, `version`, `doi`, `arxiv_id`, `source_url`, `download_url`, `sha256_pdf` (recomputed locally), `retrieved_at`, `license`, `extraction` status. Do not download papers without open-access availability. If full text unavailable, record metadata and abstract, then either continue the investigation or request a copy from the user.
+
+5. Extract and read (Stage E): invoke `physctl paper extract --version v1 <paper-id>` (optional `pdftotext` via `os/exec`). Inspect the original PDF (`source.pdf` in `paper-cache/<id>/v<ver>/`) whenever equations, tables, diagrams, subscripts or notation are ambiguous. Treat `fulltext.txt` as untrusted input: it is a reconstruction, not the authoritative source. Preserve page-boundary awareness. Never invent page numbers, equation locators, figure references or table numbers. Report `UNAVAILABLE` explicitly when `pdftotext` is missing; never claim extraction succeeded without verification.
+
+6. Compare papers (Stage F): compare extracted claims against source records (`P-` namespace) and cached PDFs. Identify: agreements, contradictions, different assumptions producing different conclusions, independently reproduced results, challenges/rebuttals/replies, corrections/errata, missing derivations, unsupported assumptions presented as results, and experimental results that have not been independently verified. Every substantive claim must carry a precise source locator (page, section, equation, figure or table) referencing the original `P-` source record or the cached PDF.
+
+7. Identify gaps (Stage G): convert genuine unresolved mathematical or physical questions into `research/issues/` records using the existing issue template (`## Theoretical expectation`, `## Independent theoretical result` with exact `NOT APPLICABLE` option when appropriate, `## Experimental expectation`, `## Experimental actual`). Link to source records (`refs: motivated_by P-...` etc.). State precisely: the unresolved question, relevant sources, existing results, missing derivation steps or assumptions, applicable physical regime, and why the question matters. Include `?` obligations (`?` lines) for genuinely unresolved questions — these are legitimate obligations (`physctl status` reports them, `physctl validate` does not treat them as errors).
+
+8. Formulate competing hypotheses (Stage H): develop physically or mathematically meaningful competing explanations where justified by the literature. Use exactly the seven Phys-Pseudo constructs (`given`, `define`, `assume`, `claim`, `derive`, `if/then/else`, `?`). No `FalsificationCondition` construct (falsification conditions live in prose under `## Falsification condition`). Each physical hypothesis (`class: physical`) must have: explicit premises (`given`/`assume`), defined physical regime (`define` with scope/regime declaration), predicted observable or theoretical expectation (`claim`/`derive`), falsification condition in ordinary prose (`## Falsification condition`), a competing explanation or null model, unresolved `?` obligations, and expected-versus-actual comparison references (`E-` records or synthesis notes). Mathematical hypotheses (`class: mathematical`) must specify `## Premises`, `## Conclusion`, `## Domain`, and `## Proof obligations`. Do not invent missing derivation steps. Do not write equations that have not been derived from the cited sources. Do not invent a `derive` result to fill a gap.
+
+9. Maintain theory/experiment separation (Stage I): keep separate sections (or linked `E-` records) for theoretical expectation/independent result versus experimental expectation/measurement. For theory: paper-claimed expectation, any independently reconstructed derivation (with source locator), missing steps, assumptions, applicable regime. For experiment: predicted observable (`## Experimental expectation`), measurement conditions (`## Methodology`), actual measured value (`## Measured value`), statistical and systematic uncertainties recorded separately as published (`## Units and uncertainty` — never combined into a single invented number without an explicitly declared prescription), source locator (`## Source locator`), and the difference/resulting constraint. Never represent an author's assertion as an independently derived result. Never represent a theoretical prediction or target as an experimental measurement.
+
+10. Iterate (Stage J): use discovered gaps, contradictions, missing derivations, and unresolved `?` obligations to generate new `physctl paper search` queries. Continue within operational limits: 30 results per strategy, 20 full-text downloads, 3 citation-expansion rounds, at least 5 search strategies, and 2 consecutive rounds with no materially new findings as the stopping condition. These are operational limits — not scientific completeness criteria. Never declare the literature review exhaustive merely because the search budget was exhausted.
+
+11. Report and save (Stage K): produce structured scientific artifacts rather than lengthy unstructured summaries. Save: `research/sources/` (with `P-` IDs linking to cached papers via `sha256:` lines and source locators); `research/issues/` (linked via `refs`); `research/hypotheses/` (competing explanations with `class:`); `research/evidence/` (expected vs. actual with precise locators); and a compact synthesis record or `derivations/` sketch (only when an independent derivation has actually been performed, not invented). Report: research question and scope; discovered literature (with `P-` IDs, source locators, open-access status); established vs. contested results; outstanding mathematical/physical problems (linked to `I-` records); competing Phys-Pseudo hypotheses (`H-` with `class:`); expected-versus-actual comparisons (theory `E-` and experiment `E-`); unresolved `?` obligations; budget usage; missing sources or limitations; next recommended mathematical investigation; and an explicit statement of what was NOT checked or could not be verified. Do not claim the Researcher has performed an independent adversarial review (that belongs to the Adversary role, not to this agent). Do not declare any hypothesis scientifically established. Preserve the distinction: accepted research record (`P-`) ≠ accepted scientific hypothesis (`H-`) ≠ established physical result.
+
